{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Close the Cloud <-> Data Center reconciler test-parity gaps\n\n## Why this exists\n\nTwo Jira adapters now sit behind one backend port, and they were hardened at different times by\ndifferent means:\n\n* **Cloud** (`adapters/jira/`, ACLI-based) is the historically battle-tested path. Years of\n  production incidents produced byte-for-byte characterization pins, ADF depth tests, comment and\n  description length-convergence suites, and a large applier/differ corpus.\n* **Data Center** (`adapters/jira_datacenter/`, pycontribs-based) was written and tested intensively\n  during epic [rebar:e369-a449-4773-48fb], which drove it against a real Dockerized instance. That\n  work produced things Cloud never had: multi-page pagination proofs, a 429/`Retry-After` matrix,\n  TLS and credential validation, a live 49-cell harness suite, and a structural anti-regression\n  guard.\n\n**The gaps therefore run in BOTH directions, and that is the point of this epic.** A parity audit\ncommissioned while closing e369 found Cloud-only coverage that DC lacks AND DC-only coverage that\nCloud lacks — and the second direction contains the single highest-risk finding, because Cloud's\nwhole-project readers have never been tested past one page while DC's have.\n\n## The governing insight\n\nEvery defect e369 found in the DC adapter was an OPERATIONAL EDGE that only appears against a\ndifferently-configured instance: index lag, server-side page caps, admin-toggled rate limiting,\nscreen configuration, workflow transition naming. None was an architectural flaw. The same class of\nedge exists on Cloud and is simply untested there, because Cloud's coverage grew from *incidents\nthat happened* rather than from *configurations that differ*.\n\nSo this epic is not \"make the two suites look alike\". It is: **for each behaviour the two adapters\nshare, the harder-won test should exist on both sides**, and where a SHARED module is exercised\nthrough only one adapter, a regression must not be able to reach production for the other.\n\n## Scope\n\nTest-tier work plus the small production changes that untested duplication forces (one map is\ngenuinely duplicated and must be single-sourced before it can be guarded). No behaviour change to\neither adapter is intended; where a test reveals one is needed, that becomes its own bug ticket\nrather than being folded in silently.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nAdding coverage for behaviours only one adapter HAS (Cloud's ADF codec depth, DC's TLS/PAT\nhandling) — those are legitimate asymmetries, not gaps. Promoting `_engine` through the mypy\nstrictness ratchet. Any change to the backend port's shape.\n\n## Provenance\n\nCommissioned during epic e369's closeout. Every finding below was verified against the tree before\nbeing written down — the audit was treated as a lead, not as evidence.\n\n## PRIORITY FRAMING (operator direction, 2026-08-02) — READ THIS BEFORE PICKING UP A CHILD\n\nThe top priority is **shipping defect-free Jira Data Center functionality.** This epic is a\n*related* priority precisely because parity is what gives confidence that the DC feature is\ndefect-free — it is instrumental to the goal, not adjacent to it.\n\nThat ranks the two directions differently, and the ranking should drive which child you take:\n\n* **DC-DIRECTION GAPS COME FIRST.** `cedc` (DC characterization) and `79d5` (DC length convergence\n  + the comment ceiling) are the ones that directly buy confidence in what is being shipped. DC's\n  values have never been pinned; its contract test asserts SHAPE only and would pass with every\n  value wrong. Until those land, \"the DC adapter is defect-free\" rests on the live harness alone,\n  and the live harness only covers the configurations that one default-configured instance happens\n  to have.\n* **BOTH-DIRECTION items rank with them** where they touch DC: `bd9e` (the duplicated ticket-type\n  map is read by DC's create path) and `2944` (several of the tests that cannot fail are DC tests,\n  so they are confidence we do not actually have).\n* **CLOUD-DIRECTION GAPS RANK BELOW.** `0fad` and `2127` harden the mature, live-exercised Cloud\n  path. They are real and worth doing — `0fad` in particular is a genuine latent truncation risk —\n  but they do not serve the DC-confidence goal, and they must not gate it. `0fad` was moved out of\n  epic e369 for exactly this reason and dropped to P2 under this framing.\n\n**Do not read \"parity\" as \"make the two suites symmetrical.\"** The goal is confidence in the DC\nfeature. Where an asymmetry is legitimate (Cloud's ADF depth, DC's TLS/PAT handling) closing it buys\nnothing.\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785687672706866001,"uuid":"f27ade08-3e44-44e7-bd47-3bd78f610dad"}